# `.githooks/local.d/` — project-local checks

Drop an executable here and it runs as part of the guardrails, in both the
pre-commit hook and the CI `guardrails` job. **`install.sh` never writes into
this directory**, so upgrading the scaffold cannot unwire your checks.

This exists because the alternative used to be editing `.githooks/pre-commit`
or `.github/workflows/lint.yml` to add a call site. `.githooks/pre-commit` is
scaffold-owned, so a re-run of `install.sh` refreshes it to the shipped
version: a call site added there disappears while the check script stays on
disk, and the check silently becomes decoration (issue #72).
The installed workflow files (`lint.yml`, `tests.yml`, `coverage.yml`,
`gitleaks.yml`) are scaffold-owned too, but drift-preserving as of #105 and
#110: a customized file is kept with a note, and only `install.sh --force`
replaces it. Put local checks here instead of either, since this directory is
never written by an upgrade, in the hook or in CI.

## Contract

A local check is any executable file in this directory. It receives exactly
what the shipped `lib/check-*` scripts receive:

- **stdin** — the file list, **NUL-delimited** (`-z`), one path per record.
  Read it with `while IFS= read -r -d '' f; do ... done`. NUL-delimiting is
  what keeps a filename containing a newline, tab, or quote from splitting the
  list and slipping a file past the scan.
- **`--ci` as `$1`** when invoked from CI; no arguments from the pre-commit
  hook. Use it to adjust output, not to weaken the check.
- **exit status** — non-zero blocks the commit (hook) or fails the job (CI).
  Every check runs even if an earlier one failed, so one run reports every
  problem rather than only the first.

Which files are in the list differs by caller, matching the shipped split:

| Caller              | List                                                                           |
| ------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| pre-commit hook     | staged files (`--diff-filter=ACMRT`)                                           |
| CI `guardrails` job | the PR/push **diff**, falling open to the whole tree when there's no diff base |

The diff scope is deliberate: installing the scaffold onto an existing repo
should not retroactively fail its pre-existing code.

Files run in glob order, so prefix them (`10-`, `20-`) if order matters. A
non-executable file is skipped — that's why this README is inert.

## Example

`.githooks/local.d/20-check-import-boundaries`:

```bash
#!/usr/bin/env bash
set -euo pipefail

FAILED=0
while IFS= read -r -d '' f; do
  case "$f" in src/core/*.py) ;; *) continue ;; esac
  # Scan the COMMITTED BLOB, not the working tree — the same thing the shipped
  # checks read, so a post-stage edit cannot hide content from the scan.
  if git show ":0:$f" 2>/dev/null | grep -qn '^from src\.web'; then
    echo "✗ $f: src/core must not import from src.web"
    FAILED=1
  fi
done
exit $FAILED
```

Then `chmod +x .githooks/local.d/20-check-import-boundaries` and commit it.

## Notes

- If you must edit a scaffold-owned file directly instead of adding a check
  here (rare, but sometimes unavoidable), mark the edit with a comment line
  reading `# Repo adaptation: <why>`. If that file is later overwritten by
  an upgrade, the installer prints a loud warning naming the line instead of
  only its routine backup note, so the customization does not vanish
  unnoticed. See TECHNICAL.md's Update & uninstall section for the full
  contract.
- Commit your checks. CI checks out the repo and runs whatever is here, so an
  uncommitted local check protects your machine only.
- The executable bit is the switch. `chmod -x` disables a check without
  deleting it.
- These run with your repo checked out and your privileges, in CI too. Review
  anything you add here as you would any other CI step.
